## 3. How the code satisfies each requirement

| Requirement | Where it is handled |
|-------------|---------------------|
| **IMAGE_DIRECTORY_ENTRY_EXCEPTION** | The exception directory is part of the PE image. When we copy the sections (`memcpy` of raw data) we also copy whatever lies in the `.pdata` (or `.debug`) section that holds the exception records. No extra work is needed – the OS will walk this directory when an exception occurs. |
| **TLS Callbacks** | `CreateTLSEntryStub` builds a small piece of shellcode that: <br>1. Walks the TLS callbacks array (read from the remote image). <br>2. Calls each callback via `call rax` (x64) or equivalent. <br>3. Finally jumps to the real entry‑point (`AddressOfEntryPoint`). The stub is executed by the remote thread **before** the DLL’s normal entry‑point, exactly as the loader does. |
| **Base Relocations** | After copying sections we walk the `IMAGE_DIRECTORY_ENTRY_BASERELOC` block, compute `delta = remoteBase - ImageBase`, and apply each relocation entry (`IMAGE_REL_BASED_HIGHLOW`, `IMAGE_REL_BASED_DIR64`, etc.). Both 32‑ and 64‑bit forms are handled. |
| **IAT Resolution** | For each import descriptor we load the DLL in the injector process (`LoadLibraryA`), resolve each function with `GetProcAddress`, and write the resulting address into the remote IAT (`FirstThunk`). This works for both name‑based and ordinal imports. |
| **General correctness** | * All Windows API calls are checked. <br>* Memory is allocated with `PAGE_READWRITE` for writing, then later the stub is allocated with `PAGE_EXECUTE_READWRITE`. <br>* The instruction cache is flushed (relevant for x86). <br>* The remote thread starts at our TLS stub, guaranteeing TLS callbacks run before the real `DllMain`. <br>* The code compiles for both x86 and x64 (just change the target platform). |

